HB_MVK_LOOP_CAP=<n> bounds every loop of the graphics stages at n iterations.

Microsoft Flight Simulator 2024's terrain fragment shader (vkd3d-proton hash 8d17e8dd824912d8) scans a bindless
table "while (entry == 0)" with no bound of its own. When it reads zeros the fragment spins until Metal's watchdog
kills the G-buffer pass ("Caused GPU Hang Error (kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorHang)", the device resumed, a
half-second freeze in flight), about once per 13,000 frames on the game's start screen on an M4. With the variable
set, SPIRV-Cross (its patch 0005) declares a counter before every loop of a vertex, tessellation or fragment shader
and breaks out once it passes n, so a spinning fragment ends with wrong pixels for that frame instead of a stalled
GPU. Compute kernels are never capped: they may legitimately run long. The cap is the last four bytes of the
pipeline cache UUID, so pipelines that patch 0012 cached under another cap, or none, are not reused.

diff --git a/MoltenVK/MoltenVK/GPUObjects/MVKDevice.mm b/MoltenVK/MoltenVK/GPUObjects/MVKDevice.mm
index 222b740..c5d8dda 100644
--- a/MoltenVK/MoltenVK/GPUObjects/MVKDevice.mm
+++ b/MoltenVK/MoltenVK/GPUObjects/MVKDevice.mm
@@ -3283,6 +3283,12 @@ static uint32_t mvkGetEntryProperty(io_registry_entry_t entry, CFStringRef prope
 	mtlFeatures |= _isUsingMetalArgumentBuffers << 0;
 	*(uint32_t*)&_properties.pipelineCacheUUID[uuidComponentOffset] = NSSwapHostIntToBig(mtlFeatures);
 	uuidComponentOffset += sizeof(mtlFeatures);
+
+	// Highball: the last 4 bytes hold HB_MVK_LOOP_CAP, which changes the MSL of every graphics shader, so pipelines
+	// cached with another cap (or none) are not reused.
+	uint32_t hbLoopCap = getenv("HB_MVK_LOOP_CAP") ? (uint32_t)strtoul(getenv("HB_MVK_LOOP_CAP"), nullptr, 0) : 0;
+	*(uint32_t*)&_properties.pipelineCacheUUID[uuidComponentOffset] = NSSwapHostIntToBig(hbLoopCap);
+	uuidComponentOffset += sizeof(hbLoopCap);
 }
 
